    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "68",
      "title": "Empowering Change",
      "description": "How a Workshop is Equipping Nursing students to Combat FGM",
      "date": "Mar 11, 2025",
      "read_time": "2 min read",
      "author": "Brenda Nyamichaba",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "68-Banner.jpg"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Female Genital Mutilation (FGM) affects more than 230 million girls and women worldwide, leaving lifelong physical and emotional scars. Despite being illegal in Kenya, the practice continues underground, putting countless young lives at risk. The act is often carried out in secret at home, or in clinics, emphasizing the importance of healthcare professionals having the knowledge and skills to identify, treat, and advocate against this harmful practice. But change is happening, one workshop at a time."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At the forefront of this fight is the "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Africa Coordinating Centre for Abandonment of Female Genital Mutilation",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "link",
                        "attrs": {
                          "href": "https://www.accaf.org/",
                          "target": "_self",
                          "linktype": "url",
                          "uuid": null,
                          "anchor": null,
                          "custom": {}
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " (ACCAF), an organisation based at the University of Nairobi and dedicated to eradicating FGM. In collaboration with the Aga Khan University School of Nursing and Midwifery, East Africa (SONAM EA), ACCAF recently held a transformative one-day workshop for second-year direct-entry nursing students. Led by Dr Joyce Jebet, a faculty member in the midwifery department at SONAM EA and a key member of ACCAF, the workshop aimed to sensitize future healthcare professionals on FGM prevention and response."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The workshop provided a deep dive into the multifaced issue of FGM, including its epidemiology and underlying drivers. Participants delved into the complex management of both immediate and long-term complications, from physical to psychological impacts. Crucial surgical interventions, including de-infibulation and other forms of care for survivors were thoroughly examined. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Furthermore, the workshop equipped attendees with essential counselling and communication strategies tailored for survivor support, while also grounding them in the legal and human rights framework that seeks to eradicate FGM. A critical component focused on advocacy against the alarming trend of medicalization, highlighting the urgent need to counter this dangerous practice. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It wasn't just a lecture; it was a dynamic space for learning and dialogue. Students actively participated, asking thoughtful questions and sharing their own insights. For many, it was an eye-opening experience that deepened their understanding of FGM and its far-reaching consequences. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Armed with this knowledge, these future nurses and midwives are now better prepared to provide compassionate care to FGM survivors, advocate for prevention in their communities, and challenge the medicalization of FGM. During their clinical placements, they can become ambassadors of change, creating a ripple effect that could save countless lives. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At SONAM EA, we take pride in exposing our nursing students to the right knowledge and skills early in their education. Workshops like this are a cornerstone of preparing them for the realities of the clinical setting and equipping them to make a meaningful impact in their communities. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
